WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.967
Question 0008: Eurovision 2026, and when it was
already clear that Israel would not win. Junior level.

2
00:00:05.967 --> 00:00:10.267
Two parts: the earliest moment,
then the code that finds it.

3
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:15.967
Every voter gives 12, 10, 8 down to 1
to ten different countries: 58 points.

4
00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:21.467
The juries, 35 countries, give 2,030 points.
The public, 36 votes, gives 2,088.

5
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:26.967
First all the jury points are announced. Then
each country gets its public points at once.

6
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:32.467
The order: from last place after the jury
to first. Once a country has them, its score is final.

7
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:37.200
Tie-break: in a tie, the country with
more public points wins.

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.167
Part A: what is the earliest moment when
we could know that Israel would not win?

9
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:48.767
The scoreboard after the jury: Israel
is 8th, with seven countries above it.

10
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:53.667
The public announces from the bottom up.
All its points, 2,088, are still to come.

11
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:58.900
The 17 countries below Israel go first:
1,040 points. 1,048 are left.

12
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:04.133
Israel gets 220 public points: 123
plus 220 is 343, and it goes to the top.

13
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:09.633
Israel's score is final. The seven countries
above it still wait for their public points.

14
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:15.100
Each of them is a cup: from its jury score
up to 343. That is its free room.

15
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.267
The water is the public points left, 828.
All of it must be poured into the seven cups.

16
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:25.500
The room in all the cups together: 1,315.
828 fits, so Israel can still win.

17
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.000
A cup filled to the brim is a tie. It gets
at most 210 from the public, and Israel got 220.

18
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.100
So Israel wins every tie,
and a cup may be filled to the brim.

19
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.600
As long as the water fits, there is a split where
no country passes 343: Israel is still alive.

20
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.600
And with more water than room, by the pigeonhole
principle at least one cup overflows.

21
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.767
Poland gets 17: the water drops by 17,
but the room drops by a whole cup, 210.

22
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:53.933
Italy gets 147 and reaches 281.
664 left, room 896: it still fits.

23
00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:59.100
Finland gets 138 and reaches 279.
526 left, room 694: it still fits.

24
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.267
France gets only 14 and reaches 158.
The water drops only by 14: 512 left.

25
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:09.733
But its whole cup, 199, is gone. The three
cups left have room for only 495.

26
00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:14.767
512 is more than 495: even the split best
for Israel spills 17 points.

27
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:19.867
This is the moment: right after France, the fourth
announcement after Israel, the loss is certain.

28
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:26.367
Why France? After each announcement the gap between
room and water shrinks by 343 minus its total.

29
00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:31.867
France finished with only 158, so it wasted
185 of room at once: from 168 to minus 17.

30
00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:37.467
Then Denmark, Australia and Bulgaria.
Bulgaria reaches 516 and wins.

31
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:43.167
In the page's box: the same board, announcement
after announcement, with the cups still left.

32
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:46.700
Poland: 811 points fit
in a room of 1,105.

33
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.833
Italy: 664 against 896.

34
00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:52.967
Finland: 526 against 694.

35
00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:56.767
France: 512 against 495,
and the line below turns red.

36
00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:02.167
And in the box's proof step, the points
are poured into the three cups left.

37
00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:06.133
Every cup fills up to 343,
and 17 points spill out.

38
00:03:06.933 --> 00:03:11.833
Part B: a function that checks one moment,
and a function that scans the announcements.

39
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:16.433
First the check at one moment: exactly
the argument of the cups.

40
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:24.867
It takes Israel's score, the jury scores
of those still waiting, and the water.

41
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.833
room will add up the free room in the cups.

42
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.600
A country already above Israel with the jury alone:
the loss is certain at once.

43
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.300
Otherwise add its cup: Israel's score
minus the jury score.

44
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:50.867
At the end: more water than room? > and not ≥,
because Israel wins a tie.

45
00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:57.167
Run it right after Israel's announcement:
seven cups, and 828 points left.

46
00:03:57.167 --> 00:04:01.567
Poland: 133 from the jury, a cup of 210.

47
00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:05.967
And so for all seven: the free room
adds up to 1,315.

48
00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:09.967
828 is not more than 1,315:
the function returns False.

49
00:04:09.967 --> 00:04:15.000
And after France: three cups and 512 points.
The room is 495, and the answer is True.

50
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:20.800
We could call it after every announcement, but
that is O(k²). Better to update two sums.

51
00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:29.233
It takes the countries above Israel, in
announcement order, and the points left.

52
00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:40.967
First the same computation of the free room,
as in the first function.

53
00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:46.833
If the loss is already certain at this moment,
return 0.

54
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.300
Now the announcements in order,
with k counting them from 1.

55
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:02.600
The water drops by the points the country got,
and the room drops by its whole cup.

56
00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:12.400
Two conditions: the country passed Israel,
or there is more water than room. Return k.

57
00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:17.733
If that never happened,
Israel won: None.

58
00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:24.033
Run it on the 2026 data: the free room
at the start is again 1,315.

59
00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:27.033
828 is not more than 1,315: go on.

60
00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:31.433
k = 1, Poland: 811 left,
room 1,105.

61
00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:35.833
Italy and Finland: 664 against 896,
then 526 against 694.

62
00:05:35.833 --> 00:05:40.233
k = 4, France: 512 left,
room 495. 512 > 495.

63
00:05:40.233 --> 00:05:44.900
The function returns 4: the loss is certain
after France, the fourth announcement.

64
00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.933
One pass: O(k) time and O(1) memory,
only two variables.

65
00:05:50.728 --> 00:05:55.828
In short: the loss is certain once the points left
no longer fit in the cups: after France.

66
00:05:55.828 --> 00:06:00.728
Try the what-if box on the page
of question 0008 on the site.
